# {{TITLE}} — Overleaf project

This folder is a complete, self-contained **Overleaf project** for the textbook.

## Open it in Overleaf

1. **Upload the zip.** *New Project → Upload Project* and select `{{SHORT}}-overleaf.zip`
   (a zip of this folder). `main.tex` is the root document.
2. **Import from GitHub.** *New Project → Import from GitHub*, pick this repository, and set
   `Overleaf/main.tex` as the main document (Menu → Settings → Main document).

Settings: compiler **pdfLaTeX**, TeX Live 2023 or newer. Overleaf runs Biber and MakeIndex
automatically.

### The figure cache (what makes Overleaf compiles possible)

`figcache/` holds every TikZ/pgfplots figure pre-rendered as a small PDF
(`figcache/ch04-idea.pdf` for `figures/ch04/idea.tex`). The style file loads the TikZ
`external` library in "graphics if exists" mode: a present PDF is included as a graphic, an
absent one is compiled from its TikZ source. No shell escape is needed.

* Overleaf premium (4-minute compile limit): the whole book compiles.
* Overleaf free (20-second limit): compile one chapter at a time with `\includeonly` (below).
* After editing a figure, delete its `figcache/chNN-name.pdf` (or the whole folder).
* Regenerate the cache locally with `python3 tools/render_figures.py`; `package.sh` does it
  before every build. A figure file that starts with `\tikzexternaldisable` is never cached
  (needed when the picture contains a `\cref` or `\label`).

### Compiling only one chapter

Open `main.tex`, uncomment the line `% \includeonly{chapters/...}` and put the chapter's file
stem there. Cross-references into other chapters show as `??` in such a build; the full
build resolves them.

## Local build

```bash
./build.sh                 # full book -> build-full/main.pdf
./build.sh ch04-slug       # one chapter -> build/only-ch04-slug.pdf
./build.sh appA-slug       # works for appendices too
./package.sh               # glossary + figure cache + full build + PDF + zip
python3 code/run_all.py    # run every chapter's code self-test
```

## Layout

| Path | Content |
|---|---|
| `main.tex` | Root document: parts, chapters, appendices, back matter |
| `textbook.sty` | All packages, colours, boxes, theorem environments, TikZ styles, notation macros |
| `frontmatter/` | Title page, copyright, about the author, preface, notation table |
| `chapters/chNN-*.tex` | One file per chapter |
| `appendices/` | Appendices; `glossary/*-terms.tex` and `solutions/*-solutions.tex` per chapter |
| `figures/chNN/*.tex` | One TikZ picture per file; `figures/data/*.dat` plot data |
| `figcache/` | Pre-rendered figures (generated) |
| `code/` | Reference implementations with self-tests; `code/figures/` generators |
| `bib/chNN-extra.bib` | Per-chapter additions to `references.bib` |
| `tools/` | `assemble_glossary.py`, `render_figures.py`, `thin_dat.py` |

## Memory

pdfLaTeX's default main memory (5,000,000 words, the same on Overleaf) suffices when plot
data files stay below about 1500 rows (`tools/thin_dat.py` thins larger ones).
